   "text": "I read the structure as a bromo-substituted rifamycin\u2013phenoxazine (a rifamycin S derivative fused to an aminophenol-derived phenoxazine), and wrote the SMILES in four parts.\n\n1. **Phenoxazine and Br.** The benzo ring carries Br para to the carbon bonded to the ring N. The NH-bearing quinone carbon sits next to the carbon bonded to the ring O.\n2. **Naphthofuranone core.** The naphthofuranone has an aromatic CH3 and an aromatic OH. Its quaternary carbon bears CH3, the furan O, the C=O and the vinyl ether O. In the drawing the CH3 is hashed and the vinyl-ether O is a bold wedge.\n3. **Ansa chain, amide end.** Starting at the amide, the chain runs C(=O)\u2013C(CH3)=CH (Z), then CH=CH (E), then CH(CH3).\n4. **Ansa chain, remaining sequence.** After that come CH(OH), CH(CH3), CH(OH), CH(CH3), CH(OAc), CH(CH3), CH(OMe), then the E-CH=CH\u2013O back to the quaternary carbon.\n\nI took each stereocentre's configuration from its wedge or hash and its position in the drawing, then converted that to @/@@ in the order the neighbours appear in the SMILES. I assigned these by hand from the drawing rather than checking them with software, so the stereo marks carry some risk.\n\nSMILES: C[C@H]1/C=C/C=C(/C)C(=O)Nc2c3Oc4cc(Br)ccc4nc3c3c(c2=O)c(O)c(C)c2c3C(=O)[C@](C)(O2)O/C=C/[C@H](OC)[C@@H](C)[C@@H](OC(C)=O)[C@H](C)[C@H](O)[C@H](C)[C@@H](O)1"
  }
